---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { dashboardUrl } from '../../lib/appLinks';

const registerUrl = dashboardUrl('/register');

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    {
      params: { slug: 'competitor-seo-analysis-guide' },
      props: {
        title: 'The Complete Guide to Competitor SEO Analysis in 2026',
        description: 'Learn how to systematically analyze your competitors\' SEO strategies, find content gaps, and identify keyword opportunities that drive real traffic.',
        image: '/hero-illustration.png',
        date: '2026-05-20',
        author: 'CrawlGap',
        category: 'SEO Strategy',
        readTime: '12 min read',
        content: `
## What Is Competitor SEO Analysis?

Competitor SEO analysis is the process of systematically examining what keywords, topics, and content structures your rivals are using to rank in search engines. It's not about copying — it's about finding the gaps they haven't filled and the opportunities they're overlooking.

## Why Sitemaps Are Your Secret Weapon

Most SEO tools focus on SERP data — who ranks for what keyword. But sitemaps give you something even more valuable: a complete map of every piece of content your competitor has ever created. By analyzing sitemap structure and frequency, you can reverse-engineer their entire content strategy.

## Step 1: Discover and Parse the Sitemap

Start by checking:
- \`/sitemap.xml\`
- \`/sitemap_index.xml\`
- \`/robots.txt\` (look for the Sitemap: directive)

CrawlGap scans sitemap URLs for a project and compares URL topics. What it can collect depends on the site's available sitemap data.

## Step 2: Filter for Blog Content

Not all URLs in a sitemap are content URLs. Filter for blog-like patterns:
- Contains \`/blog/\`, \`/news/\`, \`/post/\`, \`/article/\`
- Contains date patterns like \`/2024/\`, \`/2025/\`

## Step 3: Extract Keywords at Scale

For each blog URL, extract:
- **Primary keywords** (1-3 per post via TF-IDF)
- **LSI phrases** (semantic relatives via embeddings)
- **Named entities** (brands, tools, people mentioned)
- **H1/H2/H3 hierarchy** (reveals topic structure)

## Step 4: Aggregate and Prioritize

The real value isn't in individual URLs — it's in the aggregate. Which keywords appear across 30+ posts? Those are the topics your competitor has built topical authority around. That's exactly where you need to compete.

## Conclusion

Competitor SEO analysis at scale requires automation. Manual analysis of even 50 URLs takes days. With the right tools, you can analyze 500 URLs in under an hour and get a complete picture of your competitive landscape.
        `,
      },
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'keyword-clustering-content-strategy' },
      props: {
        title: 'Keyword Clustering: How to Build a Content Strategy That Scales',
        description: 'Stop targeting keywords one by one. Learn how keyword clustering and topic modeling help you build topical authority.',
        image: '/hero-illustration.png',
        date: '2026-05-10',
        author: 'CrawlGap',
        category: 'Content Strategy',
        readTime: '9 min read',
        content: `
## Why Single-Keyword Targeting No Longer Works

Google's algorithms have evolved far beyond matching exact keywords. Modern search ranking considers **topical authority** — does your site comprehensively cover a subject area? Targeting one keyword per post leaves massive traffic on the table.

## What Is Keyword Clustering?

Keyword clustering is the practice of grouping related keywords by search intent and semantic similarity. Instead of writing one post per keyword, you write one comprehensive post that ranks for an entire cluster of related terms.

## How to Build Clusters

1. **Collect seed keywords** from competitor analysis
2. **Calculate semantic similarity** using embeddings (Sentence-Transformers, OpenAI)
3. **Group by cosine similarity** threshold (>0.75 = same cluster)
4. **Map clusters to content types** (pillar page vs. supporting posts)

## The Pillar-Cluster Model

A pillar page covers a broad topic comprehensively. Supporting cluster posts dive deep into specific subtopics, all linking back to the pillar. This interlinking structure signals topical authority to Google.

## Tools for Keyword Clustering

- **CrawlGap**: Generates hub and spoke topic plans from project research
- **Sentence-Transformers**: Open-source embeddings for DIY clustering
- **KeyBERT**: Context-aware keyword extraction from articles

## Conclusion

Keyword clustering transforms a random list of keywords into a structured content roadmap. Start with competitor analysis to identify proven clusters, then systematically fill gaps with better content.
        `,
      },
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'sitemap-seo-best-practices' },
      props: {
        title: 'XML Sitemaps in 2026: What Google Actually Wants',
        description: 'A deep dive into sitemap structure, priority values, frequency settings, and how to leverage sitemaps for competitive intelligence.',
        image: '/hero-illustration.png',
        date: '2026-04-28',
        author: 'CrawlGap',
        category: 'Technical SEO',
        readTime: '7 min read',
        content: `
## The XML Sitemap Basics (That Many Get Wrong)

An XML sitemap is not a ranking factor — it's a discovery tool. It tells search engines about pages that might otherwise be hard to find. Getting it wrong doesn't hurt you, but getting it right can significantly speed up indexation.

## Sitemap Index Files

For large sites with 50,000+ URLs, use a sitemap index file that points to multiple sitemap files. Each child sitemap can have a maximum of 50,000 URLs.

## The Priority and Changefreq Myth

Google has publicly stated that it largely ignores \`<priority>\` and \`<changefreq>\` values. Focus instead on:
- Including only canonical URLs
- Keeping the sitemap updated with new content
- Removing 404 and redirect URLs promptly

## Sitemaps as Competitive Intelligence

Here's what most SEOs miss: your competitor's sitemap is publicly accessible and tells you their entire content strategy. By analyzing:
- URL structure (reveals site architecture)
- \`<lastmod>\` dates (reveals publishing velocity)
- Total URL count by section (reveals content investment)

You get an X-ray of their strategy without them knowing.

## Conclusion

Keep your sitemap accurate for search engines. CrawlGap can also compare sitemap URLs across a project to help you review topic coverage.
        `,
      },
    },
  ];
}

const { slug } = Astro.params;
const { title, description, image, date, author, category, readTime, content } = Astro.props;

const formattedDate = new Date(date).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' });

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": title,
  "description": description,
  "image": `https://crawlgap.io${image}`,
  "datePublished": date,
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": author },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "CrawlGap",
    "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://crawlgap.io/logo.svg" },
  },
};

const breadcrumb = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://crawlgap.io/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Blog", "item": "https://crawlgap.io/blog/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": title, "item": `https://crawlgap.io/blog/${slug}/` },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalURL={`https://crawlgap.io/blog/${slug}/`} ogImage={image}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumb)} />

  <Header />
  <main class="page-main">
    <!-- Breadcrumb -->
    <div class="breadcrumb-bar">
      <div class="container">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
          <a href="/blog/">Blog</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
          <span aria-current="page">{category}</span>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Article Hero -->
    <article class="article" aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <div class="article-hero">
        <div class="container article-hero-inner">
          <span class="badge badge-teal">{category}</span>
          <h1 id="article-title" class="article-title">{title}</h1>
          <p class="article-desc">{description}</p>
          <div class="article-meta">
            <div class="article-author">
              <div class="author-avatar-lg" aria-hidden="true">{author.split(' ').map((n: string) => n[0]).join('')}</div>
              <div>
                <div class="author-name">{author}</div>
                <time datetime={date} class="article-date">{formattedDate} · {readTime}</time>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Featured Image -->
      <div class="article-image-wrapper">
        <div class="container">
          <img
            src={image}
            alt={`Featured image for: ${title}`}
            width="1200"
            height="600"
            class="article-image"
            loading="eager"
            fetchpriority="high"
          />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Article Body -->
      <div class="container article-layout">
        <div class="article-body prose">
          <!-- Render markdown-style content -->
          {content.split('\n\n').map((block: string) => {
            if (block.startsWith('## ')) {
              return <h2>{block.replace('## ', '')}</h2>;
            } else if (block.startsWith('### ')) {
              return <h3>{block.replace('### ', '')}</h3>;
            } else if (block.startsWith('- ')) {
              const items = block.split('\n').filter((l: string) => l.startsWith('- '));
              return <ul>{items.map((item: string) => <li set:html={item.replace('- ', '').replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>').replace(/`(.*?)`/g, '<code>$1</code>')} />)}</ul>;
            } else if (block.startsWith('1. ')) {
              const items = block.split('\n').filter((l: string) => /^\d+\./.test(l));
              return <ol>{items.map((item: string) => <li set:html={item.replace(/^\d+\. /, '').replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />)}</ol>;
            } else if (block.trim()) {
              return <p set:html={block.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>').replace(/`(.*?)`/g, '<code>$1</code>')} />;
            }
            return null;
          })}
        </div>

        <!-- Sidebar -->
        <aside class="article-sidebar">
          <div class="sidebar-card glass-card">
            <h3>Explore CrawlGap</h3>
            <p>Compare sitemap URLs, review keyword opportunities, and use the content planning tools.</p>
            <a href={registerUrl} class="btn btn-primary" style="width:100%;justify-content:center;margin-top:1rem;" id="article-cta">Get Started →</a>
          </div>

          <div class="sidebar-card glass-card" style="margin-top:1rem;">
            <h3>More Articles</h3>
            <ul class="sidebar-links">
              <li><a href="/blog/keyword-clustering-content-strategy/">Keyword Clustering Strategy</a></li>
              <li><a href="/blog/sitemap-seo-best-practices/">XML Sitemaps Best Practices</a></li>
              <li><a href="/blog/competitor-seo-analysis-guide/">Competitor SEO Analysis</a></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Back to Blog -->
    <div class="container" style="padding-bottom:4rem;">
      <div class="divider" style="margin-bottom:2rem;"></div>
      <a href="/blog/" class="btn btn-ghost">← Back to Blog</a>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-main { padding-top: 72px; }

  .breadcrumb-bar {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.01);
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.625rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-text-faint);
  }

  .breadcrumb a { transition: color 0.2s; }
  .breadcrumb a:hover { color: var(--color-teal); }

  .article-hero {
    padding: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .article-hero-inner {
    max-width: 800px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .article-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .article-desc {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .article-meta { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 2rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }

  .article-author { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; }

  .author-avatar-lg {
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-teal-glow);
    color: var(--color-teal);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-weight: 700;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .author-name { font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .article-date { font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-text-faint); display: block; }

  .article-image-wrapper { padding: 2rem 0; }
  .article-image {
    width: 100%;
    height: 400px;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .article-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 300px;
    gap: 3rem;
    padding-top: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) { .article-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .article-sidebar { display: none; } }

  /* Prose styles */
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.625rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 96px;
  }

  .prose h3 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .prose p {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .prose strong { color: var(--color-text); }

  .prose code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875em;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.125em 0.375em;
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }

  .prose ul, .prose ol {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .prose ul { list-style: disc; }
  .prose ol { list-style: decimal; }

  .prose li {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* Sidebar */
  .article-sidebar { position: sticky; top: 100px; }

  .sidebar-card {
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .sidebar-card h3 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .sidebar-card p {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .sidebar-links {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.625rem;
  }

  .sidebar-links a {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .sidebar-links a:hover { color: var(--color-teal); }
</style>
